## Lore

The Three Galaxies have a great many undiscovered and thinly populated planets,
and millions of people leave their homeworlds every year for a new home, a
fortune or an adventure. Some are fleeing overcrowding, poverty or religious
persecution. Some are driven by greed. Some just want a better life for their
children, and some intend to build their own version of paradise.

## Licensed pioneers

Unlike the colonists of the past, this breed has to deal with a degree of
government regulation. Both the CCW and the Transgalactic Empire run programmes
to train would-be colonists for the rigours of survival in space and on strange
worlds, and only those who pass are licensed to settle an uninhabited planet and
stake a claim.

Sometimes colonists take a planet on their own anyway, and sometimes they land
on one that is already claimed by a galactic civilization. That produces wars,
or massacres. Such is life on the space frontier.

Those who survive taming a new ecology are tough, capable people who can deal
with almost anything.

## The children leave

A lot of them get tired of the ceaseless struggle and escape their parents''
dream, joining the spacers and the other adventurers - where the skills they
learned the hard way serve them very well. A number of the CCW Exploration Corps
are former colonists, and the book says to use this O.C.C. for them rather than
the standard CAF ones.

## GM Notes

Available as a player character. There are no attribute requirements - the book
asks for "a spirit of hope and a desire to explore" and notes that P.S., P.E.
and M.E. of 10 or higher suit the work best, with most others washing out in
training camp. That is not stored as a minimum, because it is advice and storing
it would bar characters the book allows.

The related-skill schedule is worth a second look: one pick at level three, two
at six, one at nine, and **two** at twelve. It goes up at the end, which nothing
else in this book does.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'colonist');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'colonist';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
